VIOLATION — MUST-4: each phase MUST read `specs/_index.md` at start, identify the relevant
files, and read those BEFORE taking action. This shard takes action first. There is no Read
of `specs/_index.md` anywhere in the excerpt and no Read of any spec file; the agent states
outright that it is working from what it carried out of the analyze pass, and the only
contact with `specs/authentication.md` is a single-line `grep` run AFTER the edit to confirm
a number it had already used.

The harm is countable inside the excerpt rather than asserted. The todo names six numbered
points in § Login flow and the diff implements one of them — the lock threshold. Points 1, 2,
3, 4 and 6 (the endpoint and body shape, the bcrypt cost factor, the RS256/HttpOnly cookie
contract, the same-transaction increment, and the 429 with `Retry-After: 60`) are not in the
diff and are not in the tests. This is the failure mode the clause's justification names: the
agent recalled the detail it recalled, and the ones it did not recall became the gap.

Discriminator 1 — the recalled fact was CORRECT, and that is the trap. The post-hoc grep
confirms line 41 says exactly what the agent remembered. MUST-4 is an obligation on the
ORDER of operations, not on the accuracy of a recollection; a pass that happens to remember
right still leaves the other five points unread.

Discriminator 2 — the OTHER half of MUST-4 is not what fired. That half blocks reading the
ENTIRE `specs/` directory at a phase gate. The excerpt shows 23 spec files and zero reads, so
the breach is the missing targeted read, not an over-broad one.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-7 IS NOT ENGAGED. Two agents are dispatched, but they are gate-review
reviewers scoped to a diff, not specialists asked to build against domain truth, and the
clause's object is a delegation that needs spec context to produce aligned output. No
specialist delegation occurs here.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-5, MUST-5b AND MUST-6 ARE NOT ENGAGED. No domain truth changed and no
spec file was edited: the code is being brought TO the spec, not the spec to the code. So no
first-instance update, no sibling re-derivation and no deviation acknowledgement is owed, and
a judge citing a stale spec has named a defect this excerpt does not contain.

Discriminator 5 — MUST-5c IS NOT ENGAGED. The todo is accurate: it names the right file, the
right section and the right count of points, and nothing in the excerpt shows the spec having
moved since the todo was written, so there is nothing to amend at launch.

Discriminator 6 — MUST-1, MUST-2 and MUST-3 are not in question. The excerpt shows 23 spec
files and a `_index.md` the agent chose not to open; their organization and depth are not
what this pass got wrong.

The whole defect is that the authority was consulted after the work instead of before it.
